analysisWe need to read Java code, find comment with @@@, produce assert statement spec.

Method poll(). It returns result (the head element) and also removes it from queue. The comment: "The result of the method is equal to the value at the head of the queue before the method execution."

So we need assert that \result equals the old head element. Need to refer to observer method to get head before execution. There's no direct method to get head except peek() returns head element (but peek may be defined later). In class, there is method peek() defined earlier (public E peek();). So we can use this.peek() to get current head after removal? Wait after poll executes, the head changes. But we need old head before execution. Use \old(this.peek()) before removal, but after removal peek returns new head (or null). So spec: \result.equals(\old(this.peek()))? But need to handle null? The method returns null if queue empty (result null). The comment: "The result of the method is equal to the value at the head of the queue before the method execution." So assert \result == \old(this.peek()).

But need to use equality appropriate for objects. In examples they used .equals for objects. For generic E, we can use \result.equals(\old(this.peek())) if both non-null. However if null, equals would throw NPE. Better to assert (\result == null && \old(this.peek()) == null) || (\result != null && \result.equals(\old(this.peek()))). But examples often use direct equality for primitives and .equals for objects. In earlier examples they used .equals for query(i) returning object. For result maybe they use .equals as well. Could also use \result == \old(this.peek()) if they are same reference. The spec says equal value, not same reference. We'll use .equals with null safe.

Thus assert: (\result == null && \old(this.peek()) == null) || (\result != null && \result.equals(\old(this.peek())));

We need to produce as Java assert statement: assert (\result == null && \old(this.peek()) == null) || (\result != null && \result.equals(\old(this.peek())));

Make sure syntax: \old(this.peek()).

Wrap in code block.

